	"roles": {
		"lamplighter": {"title": "Lamplighter", "work": "plaza",
			"intro": ["Mind the ladder. Mind the flame. Mind the dark, most of all.", "{name}. I keep the lanterns lit. Every one, every night. That's the whole of the job, and the whole of the village."],
			"self": ["A lantern out on the strand is a door left open. The fog comes in through it, and the fog's never alone.",
				"My father lit them before me, and his mother before him. Nobody's ever let them all go out at once. Nobody wants to learn why."],
			"talk": ["Whale oil burns cleanest. Fish oil smokes, but it scares the dead off just the same.",
				"I count the lanterns from the chapel roof at dusk. Forty-one. If I count forty, I go looking.",
				"Wicks want trimming every third night, or they gutter. A guttering lamp's worse than none. It lies to you.",
				"The fog leans on the light. You can see it, pressing, like a dog at a gate.",
				"Children here learn the lantern song before their names. Keep it lit, keep it high, keep it lit till morning.",
				"Sailors laugh at us, all these lamps in daylight. They stop laughing at about the fourth bell.",
				"Ran out of oil once, the winter of the long fog. We burned the furniture. Then the boats. Then the doors."]},
		"netmender": {"title": "Net-mender", "work": "nets",
			"intro": ["Hold that end. No, the other end. The one that isn't rotten.", "{name}. I mend the nets. The sea tears them, and something in the sea tears them on purpose."],
			"self": ["The nets come up with hands in them, some mornings. Not fish. Hands. We cut them loose and say nothing.",
				"My husband went out on the tide twelve years ago. I still mend his net. It's the least I can do. It's all I can do."],
			"talk": ["Knot, pull, knot, pull. My fingers do it in my sleep. My dreams are full of knots.",
				"Herring's thin this year. The fish keep away from the bay. They know something.",
				"Tar the twine and it lasts a season. Don't, and the sea has it by the new moon.",
				"Found a gold ring in the net this morning. Gave it back to the sea. You don't keep what the drowned drop.",
				"That tear's a seal's. That one's a rock. That one's teeth, and nothing in the almanac has teeth like that.",
				"Fog's thick enough to mend in, this morning. Feel your way and trust your hands."]},
		"gravedigger": {"title": "Gravedigger", "work": "field",
			"intro": ["Don't stand on that one. He was a grump alive and he's a grump now.", "{name}. I dig the graves. Deep, mind. Deeper than the mainland bothers with. There's a reason."],
			"self": ["Six feet's for places where the dead stay down. Here it's twelve, and a stone on top, and a lantern on the stone.",
				"I've buried the same man twice. Fished him out of the bay a month after the funeral, walking. We did it properly the second time."],
			"talk": ["Wet ground here. You dig a hole and the sea fills it from underneath. Like it wants them back.",
				"Iron nails in the coffin lid, salt on the shroud, a coin in the mouth. Skimp on any and you'll see them again.",
				"Quiet trade, mine. Steady, though. Fog keeps the trade very steady.",
				"Every stone up there faces the sea. So they can see the ship coming for them, my gran said.",
				"Lot of empty graves on the old side of the yard. Dug, filled, empty. Nobody talks about the old side.",
				"Spade's my oldest friend. Never once asked me what I saw at night."]},
		"widow": {"title": "Widow", "work": "home",
			"intro": ["Come in out of the fog. Quick, now, before it follows you.", "I'm {name}. My man went down with the Mercy. Half the village are widows. We keep each other's lamps."],
			"self": ["On the anniversary of the wreck I leave a candle in the window. Three years ago something knocked to be let in. I didn't.",
				"They say the drowned walk back to the house they left. I've moved my bed to the other side of the room. Just in case."],
			"talk": ["Bread's in the oven, the lamp's full and the door's barred. A good day, in this place.",
				"Knitting keeps the hands busy. Busy hands don't shake.",
				"I hear the bell from the wreck some nights. It rang the day they went down. It's never stopped.",
				"The young ones want to leave for the mainland. I tell them go. Go and don't look back at the fog.",
				"Salt on the doorstep, a horseshoe over the door, a lamp in every window. Belt and braces.",
				"He was a good man, my Silas. A good man and a poor sailor. You need both to drown here."]},
		"bellringer": {"title": "Bell-ringer", "work": "shrine",
			"intro": ["You'll hear me before you see me. In fog, everyone does.", "{name}. I ring the chapel bell every hour of the fog. So the boats can find home, and the other things can't."],
			"self": ["The bell was cast from the anchor of the first ship that wrecked here. It remembers the sea. It rings truer for it.",
				"There's another bell out in the bay. Not ours. It answers ours, some nights, a heartbeat late."],
			"talk": ["One for the hour, two for a boat coming in, three for the drowned on the strand. Learn them.",
				"My ears ring all day. My hands are raw from the rope. The village sleeps, though. Worth it.",
				"The bell's cracked. We daren't recast it. It might not remember what it's for.",
				"Bats live up in the belfry. They don't mind the noise. Deaf as posts, the lot of them.",
				"Ring it slow in the fog. Fast panics people. Slow tells them we're still here.",
				"I pray between the hours. Not for me. For whoever's out there in it."]},
		"eelcatcher": {"title": "Eel-catcher", "work": "boats",
			"intro": ["Mind the pots. And mind your fingers near the pots.", "{name}. I set eel pots in the black water under the dead trees. Eels don't mind the fog. Eels don't mind anything."],
			"self": ["Eels come up fat out of the drowned wood. Too fat. I don't ask what they've been eating. I just sell them.",
				"Once I pulled a pot up and there was a lantern in it, still lit, under the water. Took it home. It's still burning."],
			"talk": ["Black water's still as glass under the trees. You can see your face in it. Sometimes somebody else's.",
				"Pole the punt, don't row. Oars splash. Splashing wakes things.",
				"Smoked eel, jellied eel, eel pie. The village eats a lot of eel. Nobody complains twice.",
				"Lost a pot to something big last week. Line went straight down and stayed there.",
				"The trick with eels is patience. The trick with this place is the same, and a lantern.",
				"My boat's got a lamp on a pole at the bow. Lit day and night. Cheaper than a coffin."]},
		"saltseller": {"title": "Salt-seller", "work": "market",
			"intro": ["Salt! Fine salt, rough salt, salt for the table, salt for the door.", "{name}. Salt from the pans on the shore. Everybody here needs it. Not all of it's for food."],
			"self": ["A line of salt across a threshold, and the dead won't cross it. Mostly. I sell a lot of salt.",
				"The pans dry in the summer. Then the fog comes back and we scrape them into sacks before it settles."],
			"talk": ["A pinch for the stew, a fistful for the doorway. That's the village way.",
				"Prices are up since the long fog. Everyone's buying for their thresholds.",
				"Salt preserves fish, meat, and people's nerve. Cheap at the price.",
				"Sailors buy it to scatter on deck when they pass the wreck. Sensible lot, sailors.",
				"Grey salt's from the near pans. White's from the far ones. White's better. White's further from the bay.",
				"My hands crack from the brine. My grandmother's did too. It's in the family, like the fear."]},
